WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.601
 This is the strength test.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:05.471
I'll be testing the strength
and durability of your bayonets

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:09.442
by stabbing them into these
fuel cans three times.

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:10.643
Lyle, you're up first.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.212 line:14%
 I'm ready.

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:15.581 line:8%
I was wondering what the
gas tanks were going to be,

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:16.950 line:14%
and now I see.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.619 line:8%
These look like some tough
gas tanks to penetrate.

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:38.772
 Well, Lyle, your
initial fitout was great,

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.107
and everything held
together on this.

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:45.077
There was no issue with
reattaching it, very solid.

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:47.847
Overall, you made a bayonet.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:48.547
Well done.

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:49.882
 Thank you.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:50.950
 Scott, you're up next.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:51.717
You ready?

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:54.520 line:14%
 Don't break it.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.391 line:8%
I had difficulty getting my
bayonet attached to the rifle,

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:02.428
so hopefully it
will be good enough.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:06.231
 Well, Scott, you just
barely have it fitting here.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:09.869
It is a design problem,
but it is attached enough.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.604
I feel like it's
still safe to test.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.190
Scott, I think your
guard has loosened

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.192
a little bit right here because
of the way you had designed it.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.660
It didn't fit properly.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:37.797
So there is a little bit of
a-- a little wiggle in it.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:40.766
Overall, it still functioned.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:41.700
 Thank you.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.603
 Matt, you ready?

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.071
 No.

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:46.038
 We're going to do it anyway.

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:47.107
 All right.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:03.857
Wow.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.530
It was a big relief to see
the blade stab through like it

00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:11.264
was supposed to do.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:14.067
 Well, Matt, not too bad.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:18.037
Probably had the loosest
fit of all of the bayonets,

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:21.941
but your blade pierced
into the can very easily.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.509
Well done.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:24.443
 Thank you.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.412
 Next step is the
sharpness test.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:29.181
For that, I'm going to
turn you over to Doug.

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:31.016
 This is the sharpness test.

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:32.351
To test the edge
of your blade, I

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.787
will take your detached
bayonet and I will attempt

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:36.322
to slice across this fish.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.691
If your blades are sharp,
it should cut through.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:39.525
Lyle, you're up first.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:40.059
You ready?

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.627
 I'm ready.

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.764
I've never tried to cut a
fish, other than to fillet one.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:46.499
I'm so nervous right now.

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:54.807
 OK, Lyle, the
design of your guard

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:56.442
did actually get in the way.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:59.145
However, parts of
your edge over here

00:02:59.211 --> 00:03:01.246
did make contact with the fish--

00:03:01.313 --> 00:03:04.016
it just didn't lacerate.

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:04.950
Scott, you're up next.

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:05.718
You ready?

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.020
 Ready.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.254
I'm extremely nervous
about this slicing test.

00:03:09.354 --> 00:03:11.324
I'm not sure if it's
going to quite do it.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.432
 OK, Scott, the one thing
that I'm having an issue

00:03:19.498 --> 00:03:22.234
here is the lug that
you have, trying to get

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:23.770
a good, comfortable feel.

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.638
It wants to turn the blade out.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.140
What it did was it bruised
it on the initial cut,

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:29.808
and then lacerated
toward the end.

00:03:29.875 --> 00:03:34.146
So that's telling me that this
is not a very sharp blade.

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:35.114
Matt, you're up next.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:35.881
You ready?

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.750
 Let's do it.

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.452
There's still the
issue of the handle.

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.020
Even though he's slicing fish,
you're still going to put

00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:43.422
some good leverage on there.

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:44.891 line:14%
I pray it all works out.

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:54.433
OK, Matt, very light
blade, so the mass

00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:56.235
is not there to lacerate
all the way through.

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:58.972
But as you can see, you
definitely lacerated.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:00.772
It's a sharp blade.

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:01.874
It will cut.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.509
 Thank you.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:07.346
 Bladesmiths, the judges have
made their final decision.

00:04:07.412 --> 00:04:09.515
It's time for one of
you to leave the forge.

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:15.254
Scott, your blade
did not make the cut.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:16.522
Dave will explain.

00:04:16.588 --> 00:04:18.557
 Scott, historically,
a bayonet that

00:04:18.657 --> 00:04:20.059 line:14%
was a soldier's backup weapon.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:24.197 line:8%
It had to be fully functional,
both on and off the rifle.

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:26.832 line:8%
Due to the positioning of
the lug on your bayonet,

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:30.136 line:8%
we couldn't get it to
fully affix to the rifle.

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:32.772
And when it was in your
hand, it actually wound

00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:36.009
up digging into your palm.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:39.411
 Scott, please
surrender your weapon.

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:43.282
 My experience here was
a wonderful experience.

00:04:43.348 --> 00:04:45.217
If I had to do it
again, I probably

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:47.085
would have paid
attention to fitting

00:04:47.152 --> 00:04:48.654
the lug a little bit better.

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:52.191
But if I can give someone
encouragement to go out and try

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:54.860
something, even if they've
never done it, you know,

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.696
you can make it.

